Giant Cell Arteritis (Mild Systemic Phenotype)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Mild temporal headache, constitutional fatigue, lower threshold inflammatory markers

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-018
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of headache and severe anxiety requiring exclusion of impending visual loss.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a new, lateralized headache in an older adult. Immediate clinical review was critically necessary to rule out high-risk ischemic features of Giant Cell Arteritis (e.g., jaw claudication, visual loss) to determine if immediate emergency department transfer for high-dose intravenous steroids was required prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I've had this nagging headache on the side of my head all day, and my friend told me it could make me go blind overnight.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports low-grade systemic inflammation without visual threat (mild fatigue and subjective aches) over the last week. Tonight, the patient developed a mild temporal headache on the right side. Following a phone call with a friend, the patient experienced a sudden surge in anxiety regarding sudden blindness. Denies jaw pain when chewing, scalp tenderness when brushing hair, diplopia, or any blurring of vision.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding permanent disability.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-oriented, holding the right side of the head, anxious.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals completely intact visual acuity bilaterally. Extraocular movements are full without diplopia. Pupils are equal and reactive. Palpation of the temporal arteries reveals normal, non-thickened pulses bilaterally without any localized tenderness or erythema. Constitutional fatigue noted, but no proximal muscle weakness (PMR). Lab history shows lower threshold inflammatory markers (ESR 35).
Environmental Observations: Quiet room, lights normal, no fall risks identified.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent paracetamol use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tension-type headache with health anxiety in the setting of suspected Giant Cell Arteritis (Mild Systemic Phenotype) - Status: Stable without acute ischemia
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Optic Neuropathy (AION) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Completely intact visual acuity, no relative afferent pupillary defect, and normal fundal reflexes.

Severe Cranial Arteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal, non-tender, pulsatile temporal arteries and absence of jaw claudication.

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal palpated intraocular pressure, clear corneas, and reactive pupils.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a temporal headache, which is a classic "red flag" symptom in this age demographic demanding immediate exclusion of active cranial arteritis. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination to prevent irreversible ischemic visual loss. Detailed cranial nerve and vascular examination definitively ruled out active temporal artery inflammation or optic nerve ischemia. This critical bedside assessment confirmed the headache was benign (likely tension-type exacerbated by anxiety), safely preventing an inappropriate emergency department surge for intravenous steroids, and allowing safe outpatient management. This could not have been deferred without risking the patient's sight if true ischemia were present.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding intact vision. Oral analgesia administered with good effect.
Positive Behavioral Change: Take simple paracetamol now and rest in a quiet room with dim lighting for 30 minutes. By intentionally relaxing the muscles in your neck and scalp in a low-stress environment, the tension headache will subside, providing immediate proof that this is not a dangerous vascular event.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review first thing tomorrow morning for serial inflammatory marker testing and rheumatology discussion.

